      "title": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+67\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $67$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+67\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+67\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}+63.\n$$",
